{% from "icons.njk" import icon %}

<header class="page-header">
  <div class="container">
    <span class="eyebrow">Join us</span>
    <h1>Volunteering and internships</h1>
    <p class="page-lead">
      The EISS is run entirely by volunteers. Contribute remotely, at about a day a week over two
      months, and leave with something that carries your name: a brief under your own byline, or a
      credited contribution to the Anthology. Open to students and to recent graduates alike.
    </p>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>What you get out of it</h2>
    <p style="max-width: 44rem;">
      All four are agreed at the start and written into your objectives.
    </p>

    <div class="tile-grid" style="grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(16rem, 1fr)); margin-block: var(--space-6);">
      <article class="tile">
        <div class="tile-icon" aria-hidden="true">{{ icon("file-text") }}</div>
        <h3>Something published under your name</h3>
        <p>
          If your track produces a brief, it goes out under your own byline, as a defined part of
          the placement.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="tile">
        <div class="tile-icon" aria-hidden="true">{{ icon("book-open") }}</div>
        <h3>Credited by name either way</h3>
        <p>
          Indexing and data work produce no byline. They are credited by name on this page and in
          the project's changelog, with a link to what you did.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="tile">
        <div class="tile-icon" aria-hidden="true">{{ icon("users") }}</div>
        <h3>A conversation with the board</h3>
        <p>
          Half an hour with a board member about your own research interests, scheduled in your
          first weeks.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="tile">
        <div class="tile-icon" aria-hidden="true">{{ icon("calendar") }}</div>
        <h3>A weekly call with the others</h3>
        <p>
          The group meets for a short call each week. Missing one is fine.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <h2>What you would work on</h2>
    <p style="max-width: 44rem;">
      We match this to what interests you. Most volunteers take on one of these, and many move
      between them. Everything currently open is on the
      <a href="{{ site.helpWantedUrl }}">“help wanted” list</a>.
    </p>

    <div class="tile-grid" style="grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(16rem, 1fr)); margin-block: var(--space-6);">
      <article class="tile">
        <div class="tile-icon" aria-hidden="true">{{ icon("search") }}</div>
        <h3>Corpus and abstract indexing</h3>
        <p>
          Tracking down the abstracts of papers given at earlier conferences, checking them against
          the programme, and adding them to the Anthology so the record is complete.
        </p>
        <p class="muted"><strong>You produce:</strong> a batch of recovered abstracts, credited to you by name.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="tile">
        <div class="tile-icon" aria-hidden="true">{{ icon("file-text") }}</div>
        <h3>Researching and writing a thematic brief</h3>
        <p>
          A short piece on one of the Anthology's research themes, drawn from the papers already in
          the corpus. A brief on deterrence starts from 46 tagged papers and their authors.
        </p>
        <p class="muted"><strong>You produce:</strong> a brief on the <a href="/blog.html">blog</a>, under your own byline.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="tile">
        <div class="tile-icon" aria-hidden="true">{{ icon("settings") }}</div>
        <h3>Improving the database</h3>
        <p>
          Strengthening the data behind the Anthology: author names and affiliations, links to
          published versions, and the connections the Atlas draws between papers and people.
        </p>
        <p class="muted"><strong>You produce:</strong> a reviewed dataset the site draws on, credited to you by name.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="tile">
        <div class="tile-icon" aria-hidden="true">{{ icon("users") }}</div>
        <h3>Annual Conference preparation</h3>
        <p>
          Helping the team get our flagship event ready, from the call for papers through to the
          programme. Everyone here takes some of this.
        </p>
        <p class="muted"><strong>You produce:</strong> a conference that happened. Seasonal, and heaviest in spring.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>How the placement works</h2>
    <p style="max-width: 44rem;">
      The terms, stated in advance.
    </p>

    <dl class="definition-list">
      <div>
        <dt>About a day a week, over two months</dt>
        <dd>
          We agree the rhythm at the start, and you can change it as your term or your job demands.
          There is no minimum you owe us and you are free to stop earlier.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Unpaid</dt>
        <dd>
          The EISS has no salaried staff. Everyone here is a volunteer, the board included. Expenses
          agreed with us beforehand are reimbursed against receipts, and nothing else is paid.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Volunteering by default</dt>
        <dd>
          Most people join us as <em lang="fr">bénévoles</em>. There is no contract, you choose which
          track you take, you set your own pace, and you can stop at any point without giving a
          reason.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>A <em lang="fr">convention de stage</em> where your programme needs one</dt>
        <dd>
          French law treats a placement as a <em lang="fr">stage</em> only where it sits inside a
          course carrying at least 200 hours of teaching a year. If yours does, we sign the
          tripartite convention with you and your institution and name a
          <em lang="fr">tuteur de stage</em>. Tell us early: the paperwork goes through your
          university.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Someone to ask</dt>
        <dd>
          You get a named point of contact from your first day, and the weekly call. Between those
          two, nobody here monitors your hours or asks for a status report.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>One published output</dt>
        <dd>
          One publishable output per person. It is edited before publication, and we may decline
          one. That leaves your credit for the rest of your work untouched.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>The year at a glance</h2>
    <p style="max-width: 44rem;">
      Placements starting in January suit the cycle best. The conference falls in June.
    </p>

    <dl class="definition-list">
      <div>
        <dt>January · Induction</dt>
        <dd>
          A short shared start on the corpus, the house style and the standard we publish to. You
          pick your track and we agree your single output.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>February to April · The substantive window</dt>
        <dd>
          The protected stretch. Track work, the weekly call, and a first draft due before April.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>April to June · The annual conference</dt>
        <dd>
          Logistics is heaviest here and everyone takes some of it, ourselves included. Expect the
          writing to slow down.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>July · Publication and credit</dt>
        <dd>
          Briefs go up, contributors are added to this page, and we ask the group what was worth
          doing and what was filler.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </div>
</section>

{# ponytail: renders nothing until src/_data/contributors.js holds a name.
   No empty state on purpose: a "nobody yet" heading advertises the opposite
   of what the section is for. Add a name and it appears. (#1354) #}
{% if contributors.length %}
<section class="section">
  <div class="container">
    <h2 id="contributors">Who has done this</h2>
    <p style="max-width: 44rem;">
      Volunteers and interns who have worked on the Anthology and the conference, and what each of
      them did. Names appear here with the person's agreement.
    </p>

    <dl class="definition-list">
      {% for person in contributors %}
      <div>
        <dt>{{ person.name }}</dt>
        <dd>
          {{ person.did }}{% if person.url %} <a href="{{ person.url }}">See the work</a>.{% endif %}
        </dd>
      </div>
      {% endfor %}
    </dl>
  </div>
</section>
{% endif %}

<section class="section-tight">
  <div class="container">
    <article class="card" style="display: grid; gap: var(--space-5); grid-template-columns: 1fr; padding: clamp(var(--space-6), 2vw + 1rem, var(--space-8));">
      <div>
        <div class="featured-eyebrow">Get in touch</div>
        <h2 style="margin-bottom: var(--space-3);">Write to us</h2>
        <p class="muted">
          Send a short note and a CV to
          <a href="mailto:{{ site.contactEmail }}?subject=Volunteering">{{ site.contactEmail }}</a>,
          telling us which of the four areas above appeals to you and roughly how much time you
          have. Mention a <em lang="fr">convention de stage</em> if you need one.
        </p>
        <p style="margin-top: var(--space-4);">
          <a class="btn btn-primary" href="mailto:{{ site.contactEmail }}?subject=Volunteering">
            {{ icon("mail") }} Write to us
          </a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section-tight">
  <div class="container">
    <article class="card" style="padding: clamp(var(--space-6), 2vw + 1rem, var(--space-8));">
      <div class="prose" style="margin-inline: 0;">
        <div class="featured-eyebrow">Already started?</div>
        <h2>Start here</h2>
        <p>
          Six things for your first week, and nothing here is a test. If a step does not work, tell
          us and we will sort it out.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>You have a point of contact.</strong> They wrote to you when you joined. Ask
            them anything, including whether something is worth doing at all.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Send us your GitHub account name</strong>, so we can add you to the repository.
            Never used GitHub? Say so. We set it up with you, and it is not a prerequisite.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Put the weekly call in your calendar.</strong> It is short, and missing one
            needs no explanation.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Pick something from the
            <a href="{{ site.helpWantedUrl }}">“help wanted” list</a>.</strong> Comment to say you
            are taking it. Handing it back later is fine and counts against nothing.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Read <a href="{{ site.handbookUrl }}">the handbook</a> before your first
            task.</strong> It covers what each job actually asks of you, and where the judgement
            calls are.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Read the house style before you write.</strong> British English, and short beats
            elaborate.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>
